Looking over there, that deer was not yet fully satisfied after drinking water, when suddenly she leaped across the river
to cross. Then, from its origin, through the interior, the womb, separated into two, emerged from the womb, and fell into the source.... Meaning of the word

Tasyaha - of her; Utpattyaha - from jumping forcefully; Antarvalyaha - from being pregnant; Uru-bhaya - due to extreme fear; Avagalitaha - having fallen; Yoni-nirgataha - coming out of the womb; Garbha - the unborn child; Strotasi - in flowing water; Nipat - fell. The deer was pregnant, so when she jumped out of fear, the baby deer came out of her womb and
fell into the flowing water of the river. Note: If the woman is afraid or very emotional, there is a risk of a miscarriage. Therefore, pregnant women should be kept away from such external influences.
That which is born, fearing the fear of the newborn, is separated by its own self, and is destroyed by the darkness of the dark night. 6. Meaning of the word

Tat-prasav—due to the untimely fall of its (deer) calf; utsarpan—jumping into the river; bhay—from fear;
Kheda—from weakness; atura—afflicted; swagatan—with welcome; swagatan—with welcome; kasyanchit—of someone; daryam—in the cave of the mountain; krishna-sarasati—Krishna's wife (deer); nipat—fell; ath—therefore; cha—and; mamar—died. Being separated from her herd and distressed by miscarriage, she crossed the Krishna-Mrigi river
and became extremely frightened. Therefore, falling into a cave, she immediately died.
Then, with affection, the king, the best of kings, having seen the one who was being weighed and measured, with compassion, like a broken bond, brought the best of kings, Bharata.
He placed the dead mother on the seat of the hermitage. 7. Meaning of the word:

Tam—That; Tu—But; En-kunkam—To the fawn; Krupanam—Poor; Srotasa—By the waves;
Anuhhyamanam—Becoming distant; Abhi-vikshya—Having looked; Apaviddham—Separated from the self-born (soul); Bandhuhi iv—Like a friend;
Anukampaya—Out of compassion; Raj-rishi—Bharata—A king equal to the best sages; Aadaya—Bringing; Mrit-mata-ram—Without a mother; Iti—Thus;
Thinking thus; Ashram-padam—In the ashram; Anayat—Taken; Ap—And; Mrit—Dead; Iv—Thus;
Thus, having thought; Ashram-padam—In the ashram; Anayat—Taken; Ap—And; Mrit—Dead; Iv—Thus;
Thus, having thought; Ap—And; Mrit—Dead; Iv—Thus The great king Bharat, sitting on the bank of the river, saw his mother putting down the child who was separated from her. Seeing this, they felt great compassion. He, like a trustworthy friend, took that little child out of the waves,
and, knowing him to be motherless, brought him to his hermitage.
